cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below. It is a classical toy simulation; it reports no hardware results."
type: software
title: "qec-scaling-bench: a classical toy simulation of surface-code memory scaling"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-10-08"
license: AGPL-3.0-only
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23241631
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.23241631
    description: "Concept DOI (all versions)"
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.23241632
    description: "Version DOI for v0.1.0"
repository-code: "https://github.com/sparkainlp-x/qec-scaling-bench"
authors:
  - family-names: "Brisson"
    given-names: "Jean-François"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0000-9778-5374"
    affiliation: "Spark AI NLP, Fredericton, New Brunswick, Canada"
abstract: >-
  A seeded classical Monte Carlo simulation, run with the Stim stabilizer-circuit
  simulator and the PyMatching decoder on an ordinary CPU, of Stim's rotated
  surface-code and repetition-code memory circuits at distances 3, 5 and 7 with
  d, 2d and 4d syndrome rounds under an idealized independent Pauli/readout noise
  model (six gate/readout points plus an off-grid held-out profile with two seeds).
  It reports Wilson intervals, an IID symmetric-flip-derived per-round estimate and
  independent two-sample bootstrap intervals for distance-to-distance ratios.
  No quantum hardware was used and nothing here demonstrates quantum error
  correction or fault tolerance.
keywords:
  - classical-simulation
  - monte-carlo
  - surface-code
  - repetition-code
  - stim
  - pymatching
  - wilson-interval
  - bootstrap
  - reproducibility
  - research-software
